UMABOND – The Journey of an Indian Rubber Manufacturing Company Since 1991

Most manufacturing success stories in India start small, and UMABOND is a good example of that. The company was founded in 1991 by Jignesh Patel, who started the business with a small borrowed amount and just one hand press machine. In the beginning, the work was mostly job work for other companies, doing small rubber processing tasks to build experience and capital. Over the next three decades, this small setup grew into a full manufacturing unit that now supplies rubber products across India and to international markets as well.

The growth of UMABOND did not happen overnight. In 1996, the company entered the irrigation rubber products segment, which opened new business opportunities in the agricultural sector. A few years later, in 2001, UMABOND invested in its first hydraulic press, which was a big step up from manual processing and allowed for better consistency in product quality. By 2005, the company had moved further ahead by implementing PLC-based automatic machines, which helped increase production capacity without compromising on quality.

One of the more important turning points came in 2010, when UMABOND diversified into manufacturing anti vibration mounts. This product line is now one of their core offerings, used across construction, automobile, railway and material handling industries. Anti vibration mounts require precise rubber compounding and testing since they are used for load bearing and shock absorption in machinery, so this move required the company to strengthen its internal quality processes as well.

By 2012, UMABOND had established a presence across India, supplying to clients in multiple states. Two years later, in 2014, the company expanded its extrusion capabilities into Natural rubber, Nitrile rubber, and EPDM materials, which are commonly used across different industrial applications depending on the environment the rubber part needs to work in.

A notable change happened in 2018 when Kevin Patel, son of founder Jignesh Patel, joined the business. This brought a new generation into the company leadership, and soon after, in 2019, UMABOND set up a dedicated sales and marketing office to handle growing demand better. The company continued investing in its infrastructure, setting up a compounding unit in 2020, the same year it expanded into international business, taking its products beyond Indian borders.

In 2021, UMABOND partnered with a machining unit that included VMC, CNC machines, lathes and CNC wire cut equipment. This allowed them to handle more complex and precise manufacturing requirements in-house rather than depending on outside vendors. The same year, they introduced silicone extrusions to their product range, adding to their existing rubber extrusion capabilities.

The innovation did not stop there. In 2022, UMABOND initiated research and development work in silicone polymer synthesis, showing that the company was not just manufacturing standard products but also investing in developing its own material formulations. This kind of in-house R&D is not very common among mid-sized manufacturing companies in India, and it reflects the company's long-term approach toward product quality and innovation.

By 2024, UMABOND had started establishing a sales unit in the USA, marking a serious step toward becoming a global supplier rather than just a domestic manufacturer with some export orders. This expansion aligns with their stated goal of becoming one of the largest non-tyre industrial and commercial rubber products manufacturing companies by 2030, with a focus on sustainable development practices.

What makes UMABOND's story worth mentioning is the consistency of growth. Instead of sudden jumps, the company built its capabilities step by step, adding new product lines, new machinery, and new markets over more than 30 years. Today, UMABOND manufactures anti vibration mounts, rubber springs, silicone profiles, moulded rubber products, extruded rubber products, self adhesive foam strips and rubber sheets, serving industries like construction, automobile, railway and material handling.

